<article class="env-lemma" id="0911">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0911">Lemma <span data-tag="0911">15.28.3</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring. Let $I$ be an ideal of $A$. Let $M$ be a finite $A$-module. For every $p > 0$ there exists a $c > 0$ such that $\text{Tor}_ p^ A(M, A/I^ n) \to \text{Tor}_ p^ A(M, A/I^{n - c})$ is zero for all $n \geq c$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Proof for $p = 1$. Choose a short exact sequence $0 \to K \to A^{\oplus t} \to M \to 0$. Then $\text{Tor}_1^ A(M, A/I^ n) = K \cap (I^ n)^{\oplus t}/I^ nK$. By Artin-Rees (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00IN" data-tag="00IN">10.51.2</a>) there is a constant $c \geq 0$ such that $K \cap (I^ n)^{\oplus t} \subset I^{n - c}K$ for $n \geq c$. Thus the result for $p = 1$. For $p > 1$ we have $\text{Tor}_ p^ A(M, A/I^ n) = \text{Tor}^ A_{p - 1}(K, A/I^ n)$. Thus the lemma follows by induction. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>